WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.912
Music Module Introduction.

2
00:00:02.912 --> 00:00:06.048
GENAI 101 - Module 1: Music.

3
00:00:06.048 --> 00:00:12.767
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:15.406
Welcome to the music module.

5
00:00:15.406 --> 00:00:18.609
Over four lessons you will make four things.

6
00:00:18.609 --> 00:00:24.725
In lesson one point one, you'll write lyrics and generate a
complete song from them.

7
00:00:24.725 --> 00:00:31.860
In lesson one point two, you'll build a sixteen-bar beat
from drum patterns you sequence yourself.

8
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:37.685
In lesson one point three, you'll score a short scene with
two contrasting cues.

9
00:00:37.685 --> 00:00:47.077
And in lesson one point four, you'll analyze a real case
about AI and music and write an attribution statement for
your own song.

10
00:00:47.677 --> 00:00:50.236
Each lesson targets two competencies.

11
00:00:50.236 --> 00:01:00.957
Song composition is about prompt engineering and creativity:
turning a musical idea into a brief the model can act on,
and then making the result your own.

12
00:01:00.957 --> 00:01:07.458
Beat making is computational thinking: decomposing a groove
into patterns and sequencing them.

13
00:01:07.458 --> 00:01:16.173
Scoring a scene is problem solving and evaluation: judging
what the model produced against a cue sheet and fixing what
missed.

14
00:01:16.173 --> 00:01:25.995
And the ethics lesson builds your ability to reason about
consent, ownership, and disclosure, and to check what an AI
tells you about the law.

15
00:01:26.595 --> 00:01:32.426
One method runs through all four lessons, and through the
theater module too.

16
00:01:32.426 --> 00:01:36.212
First, write a brief before you generate anything.

17
00:01:36.212 --> 00:01:44.238
Second, change only one variable each time you regenerate,
so you learn what the prompt actually controls.

18
00:01:44.238 --> 00:01:52.568
Third, evaluate the output against criteria you named in
advance, not against how you happen to feel about it.

19
00:01:52.568 --> 00:01:59.004
And fourth, credit the tool and your own contribution under
every artifact you share.

20
00:01:59.604 --> 00:02:01.131
Two practical notes.

21
00:02:01.131 --> 00:02:02.505
Put on headphones.

22
00:02:02.505 --> 00:02:12.735
Many of the flaws you'll be asked to hear, like tempo drift,
robotic vocal phrasing, or a muddy low end, disappear on
laptop speakers.

23
00:02:12.735 --> 00:02:16.858
And keep a running document called Music Module Notes.

24
00:02:16.858 --> 00:02:22.126
Paste every prompt you use and write one line about what it
produced.

25
00:02:22.126 --> 00:02:25.256
You'll need it for the module reflection.

26
00:02:25.256 --> 00:02:32.814
Finally, remember that nothing here replaces what you know
about melody, harmony, rhythm, and form.

27
00:02:32.814 --> 00:02:42.663
The tools are fast and often impressive, and they are
frequently wrong about exactly the things a trained musician
notices first.

28
00:02:42.663 --> 00:02:47.320
Your expertise is what turns a generation into a
composition.

29
00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.221
Let's begin with lesson one point one.
